meta {
  name: 02 Get Ride (passenger view)
  type: http
  seq: 2
}

get {
  url: {{base_url}}/rides/{{ride_id}}
  body: none
  auth: bearer
}

auth:bearer {
  token: {{passenger_access_token}}
}

tests {
  test("status is 200", function () {
    expect(res.status).to.equal(200);
  });
  test("still requested, driver not yet assigned (offer is pending accept)", function () {
    expect(res.body.status).to.equal("requested");
    expect(res.body.driver).to.equal(null);
  });
}

docs {
  `driver` is correctly `null` here — an offer being open is not the same as a driver
  being assigned. `ride.driver_id` is only set inside `MatchingService.accept()`
  (`matching_service.py:130`), i.e. after "03 Accept Ride" runs. Checking `driver !=
  null` at this point in the sequence was this collection's own bug, caught by running
  it against a real local server: the server logged `matching_offer_opened` correctly,
  but this request ran before the driver had accepted, so the assertion was checking
  the wrong state for its position in the flow.

  If you genuinely see NO offer at all (check the `matching_nearby_candidates` /
  `matching_no_candidate` log lines), the real checklist still applies: was "Go Online"
  run within the last 30s, does the vehicle's category/comfort_level match the ride
  request, and is the driver's KYC actually approved (`GET /drivers/me` shows
  `status: "active"`).
}
